// A call qualified by a LOCAL GENERIC interface this builder cannot represent,
// although `ifaceinst.go` makes most of them representable.
//
// # HOW THIS FIXTURE RELATES TO `iface_cascade.nomi`
//
// `iface_cascade.nomi`'s subject LOWERS: `impl Chooser for Pair<E>`
// monomorphizes the interface at `E`'s instantiation and
// `Chooser.left(Pair{a: 10, b: 20})` becomes a direct call. This file adds the
// one ingredient that keeps the same call from lowering, a generic interface
// function, so the two fixtures together show both sides of that line and
// neither one can pass for the other.
//
// It is a separate FILE because several testdata fixtures pin absolute line
// numbers, so a new shape gets a new file rather than an edit to an old one.
//
// # THE RESIDUE, and it is real rather than contrived
//
// `Chooser.pick<K> where K: Comparable` is a GENERIC INTERFACE FUNCTION —
// `methodSignatureGap`'s hard row. The function's own type parameter is the
// DICTIONARY's population, not monomorphization's: solving `K` needs the CALL
// SITE, and an impl block cannot supply it. So the interface's requirements are
// unresolved, `inheritDefaults` refuses the impl on the method's own
// reason, and the call falls through `ifaceInstCall` (no lowerable impl) to
// `implCall`'s `useSiteOnly` suppression arm — which reports `generic interface`
// AT THE CALL, naming `Chooser.left`.
//
// `11-interfaces-and-impls/interface_defaults/interface_defaults_test.nomi`
// carries this exact shape twice (`Ranked.prefer<K>`, `Chooser.choose<K>`), so
// the residue is a corpus population and not an invented one.
//
// This program is REFUSED and never runs; there is no VM output to
// compare. What it pins is the blocker SET.
//
// The `Display.to_string(1)` beside it is the control, the same one
// `iface_cascade.nomi` carries, and it is the reason the decline lives at the
// BOTTOM of the chain rather than at the ifaceNamed arm: a module may declare
// its own unrepresentable interface and still make an ordinary stdlib-qualified
// call, and declining early would take that away.
interface Chooser<T> {
    fn left(p: self): T
    fn right(p: self): T

    fn pick<K>(p: self, _k: K): T where K: Comparable {
        Chooser.left(p)
    }
}

struct Pair<E> {
    a: E
    b: E
}

impl Chooser for Pair<E> {
    fn left(p: Pair<E>): E {
        p.a
    }

    fn right(p: Pair<E>): E {
        p.b
    }
}

fn main() {
    _ = Chooser.left(Pair{a: 10, b: 20})
    _ = Display.to_string(1)
}
